 #:seon.task{:id
             "seon.system/a-stale-binary-is-rebuilt-even-when-it-cannot-boot",
             :plan
             "What exists: bin/system start learns that its binary is stale only from the root itself. After boot, the root compares target/seon.program's tree with main's tree and prints ROOT STALE BINARY; start then runs bin/build seon and starts again (bin/system:98-116, :167-175, seon.system/binary-current). The cause: a stale binary that dies before that line is never rebuilt. Seen 2026-11-13 in .worktrees/web: target/seon was built at 02:04, main later deleted :seon.config.proofs/root-item-workers (e840e7d334), and the old binary's seon.prove/config-admission (inside seon.system/start!) threw \"set nowhere\" before the check. start reported \"root failed to start\", even after --reindex; only a hand-run bin/build seon fixed it. Correct: the binary's tree is read from target/seon.program before boot (a file read, not a tree walk). Or, if a root exits before ROOT LISTENING, start compares that file with the checkout's tree and rebuilds once. A stale binary is never the reason a root fails. Proof: a test where a binary whose recorded tree differs from the checkout's, and whose boot throws, is rebuilt and started once."}
 #:seon.task{:id "seon.system/an-update-keeps-running-agents",
