 #:seon.task{:id
             "seon.system/status-says-up-only-once-the-root-listens",
             :plan
             "What exists: bin/system status (bin/system:146-149) says \"root PID up\" whenever the pids file's process is alive (`running`). It reads root.port with `cat ... || print $PORT`, falling back to the configured port. The root writes runs/root.port only after it binds and prints ROOT LISTENING, so between a fork and that line, status says \"up\" with a port nothing listens on yet. A caller that trusts it (runtime found one) races the root. The cause: \"up\" is read from liveness, not readiness, and the fallback makes up a port. Correct: status says up only when root.port exists (written at ROOT LISTENING, removed at exit). Otherwise it says starting (alive, not listening), with no port. No fallback port and no wait: a caller that needs the root waits on start's own ready line, which start already reads. Proof: a status read between fork and listen says starting and names no port; after ROOT LISTENING it says up with the bound port."}
